WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:06.300
The latest DC superhero movie is upon us, this&nbsp;
time introducing the world to Blue Beetle. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:10.560
though he may be new to the big screen,&nbsp;
there are plenty of references to his long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.020
history hidden in the margins. These are the&nbsp;
secrets we spotted. Warning: Spoilers ahead!

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:21.840
The first character we see in "Blue Beetle"&nbsp;
isn't Jaime or the villain Victoria Kord.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.720
It's Conrad Carapax, Victoria's lieutenant and&nbsp;
bodyguard who acts as the direct foil to Jaime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:32.280
It's an interesting choice, making the first&nbsp;
face we see be that of a supporting villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.020
but Carapax ends up being much more central to&nbsp;
the overarching themes of the movie. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.900
if you've read the comics, you're already&nbsp;
well acquainted with the character.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.500
In the "Blue Beetle" comics, Carapax&nbsp;
is known as the Indestructible Man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.700
His mind becomes one with a powerful&nbsp;
robot body due to a strange accident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:52.500
making him a formidable threat to anyone&nbsp;
he comes up against. His robot body in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.880
comics is pretty similar to his depiction in&nbsp;
the movie when his full OMAC suit is activated:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.380
red in color with angular shoulder plates&nbsp;
and a massive helmet with a Y-shaped visor.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.600
Though his story in the "Blue&nbsp;
Beetle" movie is quite different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.520
Carapax retains some of his comic book essence.&nbsp;
We see how injury after injury in the service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:14.280
of Victoria led to a gradual replacement of his&nbsp;
body parts with mechanical augmentations. Slowly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.260
he becomes a full cyborg, always the one who has&nbsp;
to sacrifice for the good of Kord Industries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.060
It's a brutal twist on Carapax's comic origin&nbsp;
story, but it works well on the big screen.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.760
Carapax in "Blue Beetle" is actually a combination&nbsp;
of two different characters from the comic books.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.320
In the past, Indestructible Man has been&nbsp;
completely separate from Buddy Blank, OMAC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.000
the so-called "One Man Army Corps." The acronym&nbsp;
has that same meaning in the "Blue Beetle" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.560
but it refers instead to a weapon system,&nbsp;
an approximation of the Scarab battlesuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.700
exoskeleton that Victoria Kord is attempting&nbsp;
to mass-produce for private policing.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.540
The original OMAC is actually&nbsp;
a hero, a superspy of sorts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.080
but the name was later applied to a&nbsp;
cyber-virus that assimilates subjects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.540
into robotic warriors. The acronym came to stand&nbsp;
for "Omni-Mind and Community," the name given to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:04.980
the group of enhanced fighters. Controlled&nbsp;
by Brother Eye, an artificial intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:10.680
that circles the Earth from space, the OMACs&nbsp;
first appeared in 2005's "OMAC Project" #1.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.740
Neither of these versions is&nbsp;
really represented in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:18.900
but combining the idea of cyber-assimilation&nbsp;
with the backstory of Carapax is a nice fit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:23.460
Victoria does want to create a whole army&nbsp;
of soldiers, which is similar to the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.500
incarnation from the comics. If nothing else,&nbsp;
it's a fun nod to a deep-cut DC character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:33.060
Buddy Blank, who was created by Jack&nbsp;
Kirby and first appeared back in 1974.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.920
At the beginning of "Blue Beetle," Jaime&nbsp;
has just graduated from college, earning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.700
a bachelor's degree in pre-law. Because&nbsp;
of his family's financial circumstances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.760
continuing his education in law&nbsp;
school is out of the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.020
but we get a small glimpse of what a different&nbsp;
future for Jaime might have looked like.

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:53.940
In an early scene, Jaime has a heart-to-heart&nbsp;
chat with his father Alberto in their garden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:58.740
Alberto points out some plants that he planted&nbsp;
with Nana when Jaime was just a little kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.780
plants that Nana later salvages to replant at&nbsp;
the end of "Blue Beetle." They talk about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.280
journey of life and the importance of family.&nbsp;
It's a tender moment and an emotional high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:12.900
point of the film's first half. During the whole&nbsp;
scene, Jaime is wearing a Gotham Law sweatshirt.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:17.520
It seems likely that in another reality, Jaime&nbsp;
would have attended the school. Regardless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.960
going to law school in Gotham City is&nbsp;
a bold move. Sure, you'd get on-the-job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:26.880
experience dealing with the most corrupt criminal&nbsp;
community in the world, but is that really worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.900
the danger of living there? It's likely&nbsp;
that this detail is just a little Easter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.220
egg with no larger narrative significance,&nbsp;
but it's cool to picture Jaime in Gotham.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.320
"Blue Beetle" has become something of a bridge&nbsp;
film for DC, the point at which we cross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.840
over from the DCEU to James Gunn's all-new&nbsp;
DCU. It's an important film in that sense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.240
but it's far from original in terms of its tone.&nbsp;
Fans of superhero movies won't fail to notice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.920
"Blue Beetle" has a lot in common with the Tobey&nbsp;
Maguire and Andrew Garfield "Spider-Man" films.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.420
The goofy sequence of Jaime trying to understand&nbsp;
his powers, the evil corporation that's creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.440
sci-fi weapons for the military, the scene&nbsp;
where a hapless protagonist stumbles into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:09.240
a tech facility by coincidence and walks out&nbsp;
with superpowers — it all evokes an earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:14.220
era of comic book movie. There's even a moment&nbsp;
where Jaime's mask gets ripped just like Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.460
Parker's in the original "Spider-Man,"&nbsp;
during his battle with the Green Goblin.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:22.860
Some moments also evoke more recent superhero&nbsp;
fare. The mildly cyberpunk neon aesthetic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.060
Palmera City and the thumping synthwave&nbsp;
soundtrack will surely draw comparisons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.140
to the "Spider-Verse" films. And Jaime's&nbsp;
cosmic dream meeting with his dead father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.820
feels directly inspired by "Black Panther."&nbsp;
Fortunately, "Blue Beetle" combines all of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:40.500
disparate elements into a whole that's greater&nbsp;
than the sum of its parts. You can easily see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.580
the sources of inspiration, but the movie&nbsp;
itself feels distinctly like its own thing.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.580
"You are a superhero, cabron."

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.140
It's clear from the start of "Blue Beetle" that&nbsp;
Victoria Kord and her company are up to no good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:57.600
She's such an outlandish, cartoonish villain that&nbsp;
she feels ripped from the early 2000s. But even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.500
with Susan Sarandon's scenery-chewing&nbsp;
performance, the true villainy of Kord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:05.760
Industries isn't as front-and-center.
 
We get all the pieces, but you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.200
put them together yourself. In an early video&nbsp;
montage that plays in the Kord Industries lobby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:14.940
we see that the company has mining operations&nbsp;
in Guatemala. Victoria later mentions that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:19.740
chose Carapax as her primary OMAC subject&nbsp;
because of his valiance in "anti-communist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:24.900
counter-terrorist" operations in the same country.&nbsp;
By the end of "Blue Beetle," we learn that Carapax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:30.060
was actually abducted as a child and forced into&nbsp;
military service after his home was destroyed.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:35.520
Kord's operations in Guatemala used the guise&nbsp;
of liberation to establish a local stake and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:40.320
start plundering natural resources. The Kord&nbsp;
real estate signs around the Keys also show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.820
how active the company is in pushing out&nbsp;
the local Latino community in Palmera and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:49.320
gentrifying the neighborhood, tripling rent&nbsp;
on the Reyes family with full knowledge that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:53.520
they won't be able to pay it. And that's without&nbsp;
going into all of Victoria's microaggressions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:57.900
which just reiterate that she sees the Latino&nbsp;
community as something to be exploited.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:03.240
"Blue Beetle" is more show than tell when it&nbsp;
comes to explaining the actual powers of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:08.340
Scarab. Jaime's frantic test sequence, in which&nbsp;
he flies into outer space, cuts a bus in half,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:13.020
and blasts two holes in his family home,&nbsp;
demonstrates the exosuit's capabilities pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:17.820
well. The rest of the movie shows his new powers&nbsp;
in combat, such as his ability to conjure weapons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:22.980
from his imagination Green Lantern style, various&nbsp;
projectile devices, and deployable shields.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.420
"Whatever you can imagine, I can create."

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:33.660
Most of these abilities, like the design&nbsp;
of the Blue Beetle suit itself, are ripped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:38.220
straight out of the comics. Blue Beetle's look is&nbsp;
incredibly loyal to his incarnation on the page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:43.680
and details like his dual swords, translucent&nbsp;
wings, energy blasters, and healing factor are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:48.780
all key pieces of Jaime's comic book counterpart.&nbsp;
Because of the nature of the Scarab, Jaime is kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:52.920
of a catch-all when it comes to powers, more&nbsp;
like Iron Man than the more singular focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:57.420
of someone like Aquaman or the Flash. But the&nbsp;
movie incorporates all of Blue Beetle's different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:02.700
powers and abilities in a cohesive way, making his&nbsp;
action scenes in the third act super fun to watch.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.440
One of the smaller DC Easter eggs in&nbsp;
"Blue Beetle" is Big Belly Burger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.400
a fictional fast food chain that pops&nbsp;
up regularly in the comics and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:15.720
on-screen adaptations. First introduced in&nbsp;
"Adventures of Superman" in the late 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.960
the eatery has been a staple in&nbsp;
Man of Steel stories ever since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:25.080
while also leaking out into other areas of the DC&nbsp;
universe. More recently, Big Belly Burger has made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:29.820
numerous appearances in Arrowverse shows like "The&nbsp;
Flash," "Supergirl," and "Legends of Tomorrow."

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:35.280
"Blue Beetle" marks the fake brand's debut&nbsp;
in a live-action DC movie. A couple of Big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:39.840
Belly Burger ads can be seen around Palmera&nbsp;
City, but its biggest part in the story comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.160
when Jaime first acquires the Scarab. When&nbsp;
Jenny Kord steals it from her aunt's lab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.330
she hides the artifact in a&nbsp;
Big Belly Burger takeout box.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:51.120
"Guard it with your life, but do not open it."

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.560
She gives it to Jaime in the box so that he can&nbsp;
safely get it out of the Kord Industries building.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:01.620
Blue Beetle is a superhero with a long&nbsp;
and storied history. Jaime Reyes may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:05.880
the latest and most well-known character to use&nbsp;
the name, but he's actually the third person to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:11.580
do so in the comics. The first incarnation, Dan&nbsp;
Garret, with one "t," debuted for Fox Comics all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.560
the way back in 1939. This early version&nbsp;
gained his powers from a special vitamin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.760
and it wasn't until he was relaunched by&nbsp;
Charlton Comics as the almost-identically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:25.260
named Dan Garrett, now with two "t's," that&nbsp;
the Scarab, a magical Egyptian artifact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:29.640
was introduced. The next character to use&nbsp;
the Blue Beetle moniker, Ted Kord, was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:34.560
introduced by Charlton, but he later became&nbsp;
part of the DC mythos. Unlike his predecessor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.120
Kord never bonded with the Scarab, instead&nbsp;
using various gadgets to get the job done.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:42.480
The "Blue Beetle" movie pays tribute&nbsp;
to these previous versions of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:46.620
hero. Ted Kord is hugely important as&nbsp;
Victoria's brother and Jenny's father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:51.960
and we spend a lot of time in his Batcave-esque&nbsp;
superhero lair, complete with his old suits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:56.100
While digging through Ted's old files, Rudy&nbsp;
uncovers the previous host of the Scarab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.360
whose name is Dan Garrett. They even mention&nbsp;
Garrett being a professor, which is his job&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:05.040
and relationship to Ted in his second comic book&nbsp;
incarnation. Like his illustrated counterpart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.720
the film's Ted never uses the Scarab,&nbsp;
instead creating his own superhero devices.

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.520
While not quite Batman, and made fun&nbsp;
of in the movie for that exact reason,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:19.860
Ted Kord was clearly an impressive inventor.&nbsp;
His old lair is packed with comics-accurate Blue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:23.700
Beetle suit designs, energy-based weapons&nbsp;
created using the Scarab's technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:28.560
and various other gizmos. Not only is this&nbsp;
aspect of Ted's character true to the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:32.160
but the items themselves include&nbsp;
numerous references and Easter eggs.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:37.140
In his comic book adventures, Ted uses various&nbsp;
energy and sonic weapons to fight off bad guys.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:41.400
These devices are represented in the arsenal&nbsp;
of gear that the Reyes family uses to free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:46.500
Jaime. However, the biggest Easter egg among&nbsp;
Ted's Blue Beetle gear is the Bug, the giant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:50.580
armored, flying vehicle that Jenny and the&nbsp;
family take to Victoria's island fortress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:55.500
The Bug is a staple of Ted Kord's Blue Beetle,&nbsp;
and it features many familiar abilities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:59.760
the film. From its impenetrable armor and&nbsp;
swift flying speed to the physical design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:05.160
a blue scarab shape with six legs and two giant&nbsp;
yellow eyes, the movie version is a great match.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:11.040
Latino culture and history are important in&nbsp;
"Blue Beetle. Director Ángel Manuel Soto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:15.780
screenwriter Gareth Dunnet-Alcocer, and many&nbsp;
members of the cast have spoken in interviews&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.500
about the importance of representation&nbsp;
and authenticity in the project. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.920
support he gets from his family and&nbsp;
community is Jaime's true superpower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:25.860
and the film also deals heavily&nbsp;
with the immigrant experience.

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:30.180
Given that Dunnet-Alcocer was born in Mexico,&nbsp;
it shouldn't be surprising that "Blue Beetle"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:35.880
takes the opportunity to reference a few Mexican&nbsp;
TV classics. The 1990s telenovela "María la del&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:40.980
Barrio" is referenced several times, with Jaime's&nbsp;
family jokingly comparing him to the protagonist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:45.600
The original series follows a young woman named&nbsp;
María who's taken out of poverty and brought up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:50.760
by a wealthy businessman. In one scene of "Blue&nbsp;
Beetle," Nana can even be seen watching the show.

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:57.180
The other Mexican TV show referenced is the 1970s&nbsp;
superhero parody comedy "El Chapulín Colorado."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:01.920
The original series follows the adventures&nbsp;
of the eponymous hero, whose name translates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:06.840
to either "The Red Grasshopper" or "The Cherry&nbsp;
Cricket." Given the show's cultural staying power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:12.420
and the familiar idea of a colorful, bug-themed&nbsp;
superhero, it's a perfect inclusion. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:17.100
Uncle Rudy hacks the Kord Industries security&nbsp;
cameras, replacing their footage with claymation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:22.080
clips of "El Chapulín Colorado." The "Blue Beetle"&nbsp;
post-credits scene features additional footage.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:26.100
"Blue Beetle" evokes an older&nbsp;
era of action-adventure movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:30.900
one where corny lines, goofy gags, and&nbsp;
snarling villains reigned supreme. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:35.280
movie that loves being a movie, and it includes&nbsp;
a bunch of references to some cinematic greats.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:40.440
In a nod to "Back to the Future," Jaime calls&nbsp;
Uncle Rudy "Mexican Doc Brown," given his penchant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:45.660
for homemade gadgets. Jaime's sister Milagro&nbsp;
refers to Victoria Kord as "Cruella Kardashian,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:51.660
referencing both the famous reality TV family&nbsp;
and "101 Dalmatians." Right before Victoria's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:56.460
soldiers infiltrate the Reyes house, Nana's tea&nbsp;
vibrates slightly as the helicopter approaches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.460 --> 00:12:01.500
evoking the iconic dinosaur stomp shot from&nbsp;
"Jurassic Park." Later, during his climactic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:06.480
duel with Jaime, Carapax declares boldly that&nbsp;
"There can only be one of us." This may well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:10.980
be a reference to the 1986 fantasy classic&nbsp;
"Highlander," which spawned the famous line,

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:13.840
"There can only be one."

00:12:14.580 --> 00:12:17.220
"Blue Beetle" doesn't stop&nbsp;
at classic movie references,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:20.580
either. During his big hallway fight&nbsp;
scene in Victoria's island fortress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.580 --> 00:12:25.320
Jaime spears a lackey with one of his stinger&nbsp;
arms and pulls him through the air, shouting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:30.120
"Get over here!" as he does. It seems likely that&nbsp;
it's a "Mortal Kombat" reference, as the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:34.740
Scorpion always says the same thing after yanking&nbsp;
an opponent toward him with his own spear.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:37.500
"Get over here!"

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:41.580
Though "Blue Beetle" spends a lot of&nbsp;
time developing Jaime, his family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:46.560
and Kord Industries, it spends much less time&nbsp;
explaining the Scarab itself. We're told that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:50.160
it's some kind of world-destroying&nbsp;
weapon, that it's of alien origin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:55.500
and that the sentience within it is called Khaji&nbsp;
Da. Beyond that, though, no real info is revealed.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:13:00.000
If you've read the "Blue Beetle" comics, then&nbsp;
you'll know all about the Reach, the alien race&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:04.920
of violent conquerors who created and sent the&nbsp;
Scarab. You'll also know that Khaji Da's purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:09.420
was to conquer Earth in the name of the Reach.&nbsp;
If you're coming to the film more casually, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:14.460
may have no idea that the device is so sinister,&nbsp;
though the few clues we do get point to this fact.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:19.020
As of now, it's unclear how closely the&nbsp;
DCU Scarab will resemble its comic book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:23.280
counterpart in origin and purpose. That&nbsp;
will be a subject for the hypothetical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:27.780
sequel to address. However, Jaime's cosmic&nbsp;
dream sequence at the end of "Blue Beetle"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:32.040
hints at Khaji Da's greater powers. Is this&nbsp;
moment a glimpse of the Scarab's abilities?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:36.780
Will it have some connection to the DCU's&nbsp;
version of the Reach? Only time will tell.

